component SigilImplementationOwnership {
  goal {
    Provide the shared core query that returns the implementation targets owned by a Sigil contract.

    Keep contract-to-implementation ownership deterministic and reusable for CLI and LSP without adding new Sigil syntax.
  }

  interface {
    OwnedImplementationLookup {
      ownedImplementationTargetsFor(resolvedWorkspace, componentName, conceptName?) returns an owned-implementation projection or undefined

      The query is contract-centric and uses an optional concept scope when a contract concept owns a narrower set of targets.
    }

    OwnedImplementationProjection {
      includes the owning contract, optional concept scope, normalized implementation targets, and diagnostics
    }

    OwnedImplementationTarget {
      each target identifies one owned implementation artifact

      each target preserves the artifact kind, file path, and optional symbol identity when available
    }
  }
}

expand SigilImplementationOwnership {
  logic {
    OwnedImplementationProjection {
      resolve results from the selected Sigil contract and optional concept scope

      normalize code, tests, and agent-facing instruction or workflow Markdown targets into one shared result shape

      prefer symbol-level targets when available and fall back to file-level targets when necessary
    }
  }

  constraints {
    ContractOwnership {
      The Sigil contract is the owner of the implementation relationship.

      CLI and LSP consume the shared core query rather than maintaining separate ownership maps.
    }

    ImplementationTargetScope {
      v1 includes code and tests.

      v1 includes Markdown files only when they are agent-facing instructions or workflow documents.

      general prose documentation, configs, and non-text artifacts remain out of scope.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      Prefer symbol-level targets when available and fall back to file-level targets when symbol resolution is unavailable.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ContractOwnership {
      Decision: Model implementation ownership as a contract-owned core query so one Sigil contract can list the implementation targets it owns.

      Scope: Governs shared CLI and LSP ownership lookup for a selected contract and optional concept; excludes new Sigil syntax and persisted anchor storage.

      Trade-offs: Contract-centric ownership is easier to review from the Sigil side, but implementation-first lookup is deferred.

      Consequences: CLI and LSP can share one authoritative ownership view while the language grammar stays unchanged.

      Revisit when: We need implementation-first navigation or persisted ownership storage.
    }

    ScopeSelection {
      Decision: Allow an optional concept scope so a contract can return all owned targets or just the subset governed by one concept.

      Scope: Governs query scoping within one contract; excludes cross-contract aggregation and transitive ownership inference.

      Trade-offs: Concept scoping increases precision without forcing separate query shapes, but callers must supply the correct concept when they need a narrower view.

      Consequences: The same API serves whole-contract and concept-specific ownership views.

      Revisit when: Concept scoping proves too coarse or too fine for routine use.
    }

    ImplementationTargetScope {
      Decision: Limit v1 implementation targets to code, tests, and agent-facing instruction or workflow Markdown.

      Scope: Governs which repository artifacts may appear in owned-implementation results; excludes general prose documentation, configs, and non-text artifacts.

      Trade-offs: The scope captures behavior-shaping Markdown that matters to agent workflows, but intentionally leaves ordinary documentation out.

      Consequences: The feature stays focused on implementation-bearing material while still covering instruction docs.

      Revisit when: Other artifact classes become meaningful implementation evidence.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      Decision: Prefer symbol-level targets when available and fall back to file-level targets when symbol resolution is unavailable.

      Scope: Governs how owned implementation targets are normalized inside the query result; excludes cross-file inference and human approval.

      Trade-offs: Symbol-level precision improves navigation, but file fallback keeps the feature useful for less structured artifacts.

      Consequences: The same query can link source symbols, test symbols, and file-level Markdown targets.

      Revisit when: Symbol resolution becomes too noisy or file-level targets are insufficient.
    }
  }

  cases {
    OwnedImplementationLookup {
      A contract query for a TypeScript component returns its code and test targets.

      A contract query for a contract with an agent-facing Markdown workflow doc returns that Markdown file as an implementation target.

      A concept-scoped query returns only the targets owned by that concept.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      A symbol-level implementation target carries a symbol identity instead of only a file path.

      A Markdown instruction file without symbols falls back to a file-level target.
    }
  }
}
